$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM 1,4-Dihydoanisole 43. A three3-necked round-bottomed flask was fitted
with a dry ice condenser and flame dried under vacuum. The flask was then
cooled to -78 °C (acetone, dry ice) and ammonia (25 mL) was then
distilled into the flask. Lithium (7.5 mmol, 52 mg) was added and once
the metal had dissolved the carbamate 42 (70 mg, 0.15 mmol), in dry THF
(2 mL) and EtOH (1 mL), was added slowly by syringe. Once the addition
was complete, the syringe was rinsed with THF (1 mL) and this was then
added to the flask. The temperature was then raised to -40 °C (acetone,
dry ice) and the mixture was stirred for 3 h at this temperature. If the
blue color dissipated during this time, then more small pieces of lithium
were added. After 3 h MeOH (2 mL) was slowly added, and once the blue
color had dissipated the ice bath was removed and the ammonia allowed to
evaporate under a stream of argon. H2O (5 mL) was added, followed by Et2O
(5 mL), and the organic layer was then washed with H2O and brine. The
aqueous layers were re-extracted with EtOAc (2 x 5 mL), the combined
organic layers were then dried over MgSO4 and filtered, and the solvent
was removed under reduced pressure to give the crude 1,4- dihydroanisole
43 (70 mg, ~100%) as a white solid, which was used immediately in the
next reaction. 1H NMR (300 MHz, CDCl3) key signals δ 4.86 (1H, br t,
-NHCO2Me), 4.56 (1H, br t, H5), 3.65 (3H, s), 3.53 (3H, s), 3.08 (1H, 1H,
dd, J = 5.1 Hz, J = 13.7 Hz), 0.93 (3H, s), 0.84 (9H, s), -0.002, -0.03
(6H, s).
