$DATUM 75-65-0
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: 1. Improved Birch reduction of o, m, and
p-Xylenes: When the procedure, which was described earlier1, was used,
the yields of the dienes were low and were contaminated with major
amounts of unchanged starting materials. The improved procedure was as
follows: To 300 mL of liquid ammonia cooled on dry ice-acetone bath were
slowly added: Ether (22 mL), t-butanol (120 mL) and 12.4 g (0.115 mole)
of one of the xylenes. Lithium ribbon (6 g) was introduced in small
pieces during 6 hours. The solution, which was blue during the addition,
was left at -78º for additional 5 hrs. Most of the ammonia evaporated
upon stirring overnight at room temperature. Water (100 mL) was added and
the product extracted 3 times with petroleum ether (80 mL). The petroleum
ether was dried on MgSO4 and evaporated in vacuum. The products (95%
yield) were pure by NMR spectra, no traces of aromatic protons were
observed. 1,2-dimethyl-1,4-cyclohexadiene 1H NMR (CDCl3), δ: 5.68 (br s,
2H, =CH), 2.59 (br s, 4H, CH2), 1.63 (s, 6H, 2Me).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM Birch redn.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Ozonolysis of 1,4-Cyclohexadienes in the Presence of Methanol and Acid.
Mechanism and Intermediates in the Conversion of 1,4-Cyclohexadiene
Derivatives to β-Keto Esters
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Gbara-Haj-Yahia, Isra; Zvilichovsky, Gury; Seri, Noa
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of Organic Chemistry (2004), 69(12), 4135-4139

