$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Table 1 Entry 4: Reductive alkylation of Methyl
3-(tert-Butyldimethylsilyloxymethyl)-benzoate (9) with 1-iodopropane. A
mixture of 560 mg (2 mmol) of ester 9 and 148 mg (2 mmol) of tert-butyl
alcohol was placed in a dry 100 mL 3-necked round bottom flask equipped
with a magnetic stir bar, a cold finger condenser topped with a nitrogen
line, a low temperature thermometer, and a septum. The system was placed
under a nitrogen atmosphere and dry tetrahydrofuran (12 mL) was added via
syringe. The septum was replaced with a gas inlet adapter attached to an
ammonia tank. The cold finger condenser was charged with dry ice acetone
and the reaction flask was also cooled in a dry ice-acetone bath. Ammonia
(approximately 35-40 mL) was condensed in the reaction vessel over a
period of about 10 min and then the gas inlet adapter was replaced with a
glass stopper. The stirred solution was cooled to an internal temperature
of -65 °C and 54 mg (7.6 mmol) of lithium metal was added in small pieces
(25 mg + 15 mg + 14 mg = 54 mg), through the stoppered hole of the
reaction flask, over a period of 22 min. The solution maintained a deep
blue color for 60 min after addition of the last piece of lithium at
which point 1.02 mg (6 mmol) of freshly distilled 1-iodopropane was added
via syringe through the stoppered hole of the reaction flask. The blue
color dissipated after addition of the first four drops of the
1-iodopropane after which the remaining halide was added in one portion.
The mixture was stirred for 30 min at -65 °C and 321 mg (6 mmol) of solid
ammonium chloride was added through the stoppered hole. The cold finger
condenser was quickly removed (after removing the nitrogen line),
emptied, returned to the reaction flask (without the nitrogen line), and
the cold bath was removed. The stirred solution was allowed to warm to
approximately -28 °C over a period of 25 min. A water bath was placed
under the reaction flask and the reaction temperature increased to 15 °C
over a period of 40 min. To the residue was added 15 mL of water followed
by 30 mL of benzene. The phases were separated and the organic phase was
dried (Na2SO4) and concentrated in vacuo (25-45 torr at a temperature
below 40 °C) to give 663 mg of a colorless liquid. The residue was
purified by chromatography over 16 g of flash silica gel (eluted with
hexanes-ethyl acetate = 20:1) with collection of 10 mL fractions.
Fractions 5-9 were pooled and concentrated in vacuo to give 542 mg of
29d. 542 mg (83%) of 29d as a colorless oil IR (neat) 1734 cm−1; 1H NMR
(C6D6, 400 MHz) δ 0.0 (two s, 6H), 0.75 (t, J = 7 Hz, 3H), 0.90 (s, 9H),
1.24 (m, 2H), 1.70 (m, 2H), 2.40 (br s, 2H), 3.28 (s, 3H), 3.90 (s, 2H),
5.7 (dt, J = 10, 3 Hz, 1H), 5.85 (dq, J = 10, 2 Hz, 1H), 5.9 (br s, 1H);
13C NMR (C6D6, 100 MHz) δ −5.2, 14.4, 18.0, 18.4, 26.0, 26.9, 42.5, 49.1,
51.3, 67.0, 122.5, 125.0, 128.0, 136.1, 174.6; exact mass calcd for
C18H32O3Si (M + Na)+ m/z 347.2018, found m/z 347.2011.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM incremental addition (Li in stage 2)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Birch Reductive Alkylation of Methyl m-(Hydroxymethyl)benzoate
Derivatives and the Behavior of o- and p-(Hydroxymethyl)benzoates under
Reductive Alkylation Conditions
