$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Methyl 5-Methoxy-1,3-dimethyl-2,5-cyclohexadien-1-carboxylate (36). A
mixture of 180 mg (1 mmol) of methyl 5-methoxy-3-methylbenzoate and 74 mg
(1 mmol) of tert-butyl alcohol was placed in a dry 50 mL 3-necked round
bottom flask equipped with a magnetic stir bar, a cold finger condenser
topped with a nitrogen line, a low temperature thermometer, and a septum.
The system was placed under a nitrogen atmosphere and dry tetrahydrofuran
(7 mL) was added via syringe. The septum was replaced with a gas inlet
adapter attached to an ammonia tank. The cold finger condenser was
charged with dry ice-acetone and the reaction flask was also cooled in a
dry ice-acetone bath. Ammonia (approximately 20 mL) was condensed in the
reaction vessel over a period of about 10 min and then the gas inlet
adapter was replaced with a glass stopper. The stirred solution was
cooled to an internal temperature of -65 °C and 32 mg (4.6 mmol) of
lithium metal was added in small pieces (16 mg + 16 mg = 32 mg), through
the stoppered hole of the reaction flask, over a period of 20 min. The
solution maintained a deep blue color after addition of the last piece of
lithium for 30 min at which point 426 mg (3 mmol) of iodomethane was
added via syringe through the stoppered hole of the reaction flask. The
blue color dissipated upon addition of iodomethane. The mixture was
stirred for 30 min at -65 °C and 160 mg (3 mmol) of solid ammonium
chloride was added through the stoppered hole. The cold finder condenser
was quickly removed (after removing the nitrogen line), emptied, and
returned to the reaction flask (without the nitrogen line), and the cold
bath was removed. The stirred solution was allowed to warm to
approximately -28 °C over a period of 10 min. A water bath was placed
under the reaction flask and the reaction temperature increased to 15 °C
over a period of 50 min. To the residue was added 15 mL of water followed
by 30 mL of benzene. The phases were separated and the organic phase was
dried (Na2SO4) and concentrated in vacuo (25-45 torr at a temperature
below 40 °C) to give 188 mg of a colorless residue. The residue was
subjected to chromatography over 16 g of flash silica gel (loaded and
eluted with hexanes-ethyl acetate = 10:1) with collection of 10 mL
fractions. Fractions 7-12 were pooled and concentrated in vacuo to give
158 mg of 36. 158 mg (80%) of 36 as a colorless oil IR (neat) 1731 cm−1;
1H NMR (C6D6, 400 MHz) δ 1.48 (s, 6H), 2.54 (d, J = 21 Hz, 1H), 2.62 (d,
J = 21 Hz, 1H), 3.25 (s, 3H), 3.36 (s, 3H), 4.82 (s, 1H), 5.6 (s, 1H);
13C NMR (C6D6, 100 MHz) δ 22.4, 28.6, 33.6, 46.9, 51.4, 53.6, 96.9,
124.3, 130.6, 154.1, 175.9; exact mass calcd for C11H16O3 (M + Na)+ m/z
219.0997, found m/z 219.0990.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM incremental addition (Li in stage 2)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Birch Reductive Alkylation of Methyl m-(Hydroxymethyl)benzoate
Derivatives and the Behavior of o- and p-(Hydroxymethyl)benzoates under
Reductive Alkylation Conditions
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Fretz, Samuel J.; Hadad, Christopher M.; Hart, David J.; Vyas, Shubham;
Yang, Dexi
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of Organic Chemistry (2013), 78(1), 83-92

