$DATUM Methyl 1,3-Dimethyl-2,5-cyclohexadien-1-carboxylate (26). This compound
was prepared for comparison by a variation of a known procedure.20 A
mixture of 1.50 g (10 mmol) of ester 17 and 0.74 g (10 mmol) of
tert-butyl alcohol was placed in a dry 250 mL 3-necked round bottom flask
equipped with a magnetic stir bar, a cold finger condenser topped with a
nitrogen line, a low temperature thermometer, and a septum. The system
was placed under a nitrogen atmosphere and dry tetrahydrofuran (25 mL)
was added via syringe. The septum was replaced with a gas inlet adapter
attached to an ammonia tank. The cold finger condenser was charged with
dry ice-acetone and the reaction flask was also cooled in a dry
ice-acetone bath. Ammonia (approximately 100 mL) was condensed in the
reaction vessel over a period of about 20 min and then the gas inlet
adapter was replaced with a glass stopper. The stirred solution was
cooled to an internal temperature of -65 °C and 159 mg (22.7 mmol) of
lithium metal was added in three pieces through the stoppered hole of the
reaction flask over a period of 7 min. The solution maintained a deep
blue color after addition of the last piece of lithium for 10 min at
which point 4.26 g (30 mmol) of iodomethane was added via syringe through
the stoppered hole of the reaction flask. The blue color dissipated after
addition of 3-4 drops of iodomethane after which the remaining halide was
added over a period of 1 min. The mixture was stirred for 50 min at -65
°C and 1.6 g (30 mmol) of solid ammonium chloride was added through the
stoppered hole. The cold finder condenser was quickly removed (after
removing the nitrogen line), emptied, and returned to the reaction flask
(without the nitrogen line), and the cold bath was removed. The stirred
solution was allowed to warm to approximately -285 °C over a period of 20
min. A water bath was placed under the reaction flask and the reaction
temperature increased to 15 °C over a period of 100 min. To the residue
was added 40 mL of water followed by 80 mL of benzene. The phases were
separated and the organic phase was dried (Na2SO4) and concentrated in
vacuo (25-45 torr at a temperature below 40 °C) to give 1.49 g of a
colorless liquid. 1H NMR analysis indicated this material was reasonably
pure 26. The residue was subjected to chromatography over 18 g of flash
silica gel (loaded and eluted with hexanes-ethyl acetate = 10:1) with
collection of 10 mL fractions. Fractions 5-10 were pooled and
concentrated in vacuo to give 1.3 g of 26. 1.3 g (78%) of 26 as a
colorless oil IR (neat) 1734 cm−1; 1H NMR (C6D6, 400 MHz) δ 1.37 (s, 3H),
1.5 (s, 3H), 2.22 (d, J = 22 Hz, 1H), 2.29 (d, J = 22 Hz, 1H) 3.30 (s,
3H), 5.6 (m, 2H), 5.87 (dq, J = 10, 2 Hz, 1H); 13C NMR (C6D6, 100 MHz) δ
23.0, 27.6, 30.7, 45.3, 51.4, 124.0, 129.1, 131.5, 175.1. One vinylic
carbon signal was obscured.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM incremental addition (Li in stage 2)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
