$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Table 1 Entry 2: Reductive alkylation of Methyl
3-(tert-Butyldimethylsilyloxymethyl)-benzoate (9) with allyl bromide. A
mixture of 560 mg (2 mmol) of ester 9 and 148 mg (2 mmol) of tert-butyl
alcohol was placed in a dry 100 mL 3-necked round bottom flask equipped
with a magnetic stir bar, a cold finger condenser topped with a nitrogen
line, a low temperature thermometer, and a septum. The system was placed
under a nitrogen atmosphere and dry tetrahydrofuran (7 mL) was added via
syringe. The septum was replaced with a gas inlet adapter attached to an
ammonia tank. The cold finger condenser was charged with dry ice-acetone
and the reaction flask was also cooled in a dry ice-acetone bath. Ammonia
(approximately 35-40 mL) was condensed in the reaction vessel over a
period of about 10 min and then the gas inlet adapter was replaced with a
glass stopper. The stirred solution was cooled to an internal temperature
of -65 °C and 36 mg (5.14 mmol) of lithium metal was added in small
pieces (17 mg + 15 mg + 4 mg = 36 mg), through the stoppered hole of the
reaction flask, over a period of 43 min. The solution maintained a deep
blue color for 32 min after addition of the last piece of lithium at
which point 726 mg (6 mmol) of freshly distilled allyl bromide was added
via syringe through the stoppered hole of the reaction flask. The blue
color dissipated after addition of the first four drops of allyl bromide
after which the remaining halide was added in one portion. The mixture
was stirred for 50 min at -65 °C and 321 mg (6 mmol) of solid ammonium
chloride was added through the stoppered hole. The cold finger condenser
was quickly removed (after removing the nitrogen line), emptied, returned
to the reaction flask (without the nitrogen line), and the cold bath was
removed. The stirred solution was allowed to warm to approximately -25 °C
over a period of 25 min. A water bath was placed under the reaction flask
and the reaction temperature increased to 15 °C over a period of 40 min.
To the residue was added 15 mL of water followed by 30 mL of benzene. The
phases were separated and the organic phase was dried (Na2SO4) and
concentrated in vacuo (25-45 torr at a temperature below 40 °C) to give
600 mg of a colorless liquid. 1H NMR analysis indicated this material was
and 8:1 mixture of 29b and 9, respectively. The residue was purified by
chromatography over 18 g of flash silica gel (loaded and eluted with
hexanes-ethyl acetate = 10:1) with collection of 10 mL fractions.
Fractions 5-8 were pooled and concentrated in vacuo to give 508 mg of an
8:1 molar ratio of 29b (71%) and 9 (9%) as a colorless oil. The presence
of 9 (11-12 mol%) was apparent from 1H NMR signals and appropriate 13C
NMR signals. 508 mg of an 8:1 molar ratio of 29b (71%) and 9 (9%) as a
colorless oil IR (neat) 1732 cm−1; 1H NMR (C6D6, 400 MHz) δ 0.0 (two s,
6H), 0.91 (s, 9H), 2.40 (br s, 2H), 2.5 (d, J = 7 Hz, 2H), 3.26 (s, 3H),
3.90 (s, 2H), 4.95 (br s, 1H), 5.0 (d, J = 6 Hz, 1H), 5.65−5.95 (m, 4H);
13C NMR (C6D6, 100 MHz) δ −5.0, 18.4, 26.0, 26.8, 44.9, 48.8, 51.4, 66.8,
118.0, 122.2, 125.2, 130.5, 133.7, 136.3, 174.0; exact mass calcd for
C18H30O3Si (M + Na)+ m/z 345.1861, found m/z 345.1853. The presence of 9
(11−12 mol %) was apparent from 1H NMR signals at δ −0.1, 3.45, and 4.45
and appropriate 13C NMR signals.
