$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Table 1 Entry 6: Reductive alkylation of Methyl
3-(tert-Butyldimethylsilyloxymethyl)-benzoate (9) with 2-iodopropane. A
mixture of 280 mg (1 mmol) of ester 9 and 74 mg (1mmol) of tert-butyl
alcohol was placed in a dry 100 mL 3-necked round bottom flask equipped
with a magnetic stir bar, a cold finger condenser topped with a nitrogen
line, a low temperature thermometer, and a septum. The system was placed
under a nitrogen atmosphere and dry tetrahydrofuran (7 mL) was added via
syringe. The septum was replaced with a gas inlet adapter attached to an
ammonia tank. The cold finger condenser was charged with dry ice-acetone
and the reaction flask was also cooled in a dry ice-acetone bath. Ammonia
(approximately 20 mL) was condensed in the reaction vessel over a period
of about 10 min and then the gas inlet adapter was replaced with a glass
stopper. The stirred solution was cooled to an internal temperature of
-65 °C and 33 mg (4.7 mmol) of lithium metal was added in small pieces
(11 mg + 11 mg + 11 mg = 33 mg), through the stoppered hole of the
reaction flask, over a period of 52 min. The solution maintained a deep
blue color for 10 min after addition of the last piece of lithium at
which point 510 mg (3 mmol) of 2-iodopropane was added via syringe
through the stoppered hole of the reaction flask. The blue color
dissipated after addition of the first 50 μL of halide after which the
remaining halide was added in one portion. The mixture was stirred for 30
minat -65 °C and 160 mg (3 mmol) of solid ammonium chloride was added
through the stoppered hole. The cold finger condenser was quickly removed
(after removing the nitrogen line), emptied, returned to the reaction
flask (without the nitrogen line), and the cold bath was removed. The
stirred solution was allowed to warm to approximately -25 °C over a
period of 10 min. A water bath was placed under the reaction flask and
the reaction temperature increased to 15 °C over a period of 20 min. To
the residue was added 20 mL of water followed by 35 mL of benzene. The
phases were separated and the organic phase was dried (Na2SO4) and
concentrated in vacuo (25-45 torr at a temperature below 40 °C) to give
353 mg of a colorless liquid. 1H NMR analysis indicated this material was
mainly 29f. The residue was purified by chromatography over 17 g of flash
silica gel (loaded and eluted with hexanes-ethyl acetate = 20:1) with
collection of 10 mL fractions. Fractions 5-7 were pooled and concentrated
in vacuo to give 220 mg of 29f. 220 mg (68%) of 29f as a colorless oil IR
(neat) 1732 cm−1; 1H NMR (C6D6, 400 MHz) δ 0.00 (two s, 6H), 0.84 (d, J =
7 Hz, 3H), 0.86 (d, J = 7 Hz, 3H), 0.9 (s, 9H), 2.2 (septet, J = 7 Hz,
1H), 2.4 (br s, 2H), 3.28 (s, 3H), 3.91 (s, 2H), 5.75 (dt, J = 10, 3 Hz,
1H), 5.87 (dq, J = 10, 2 Hz, 1H), 5.92 (br s, 1H); 13C NMR (C6D6, 100
MHz) δ −5.34, −5.31, 17.52, 17.55, 18.4, 25.9, 27.2, 36.4, 51.2, 53.0,
66.9, 120.9, 125.9, 126.4, 137.0, 174.5; exact mass calcd for C18H32O3Si
(M + Na)+ m/z 347.2018, found m/z 347.2012.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM incremental addition (Li in stage 2)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Birch Reductive Alkylation of Methyl m-(Hydroxymethyl)benzoate
Derivatives and the Behavior of o- and p-(Hydroxymethyl)benzoates under
Reductive Alkylation Conditions
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Fretz, Samuel J.; Hadad, Christopher M.; Hart, David J.; Vyas, Shubham;
Yang, Dexi
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of Organic Chemistry (2013), 78(1), 83-92

$RFMT $RIREG SCHEME354 STEP1
