$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Table 1 Entry 8: Reductive alkylation of Methyl 3-Hydroxymethylbenzoate
(11) with iodomethane. A mixture of 332 mg (2 mmol) of ester 11 and 296
mg (4 mmol) of tert-butyl alcohol was placed in a dry 100 mL 3-necked
round bottom flask equipped with a magnetic stir bar, a cold finger
condenser topped with a nitrogen line, a low temperature thermometer, and
a septum. The system was placed under a nitrogen atmosphere and dry
tetrahydrofuran (6 mL) was added via syringe. The septum was replaced
with a gas inlet adapter attached to an ammonia tank. The cold finger
condenser was charged with dry ice-acetone and the reaction flask was
also cooled in a dry ice-acetone bath. Ammonia (approximately 35 mL) was
condensed in the reaction vessel over a period of about 10 min and then
the gas inlet adapter was replaced with a glass stopper. The stirred
solution was cooled to an internal temperature of -65 °C and 68 mg (9.7
mmol) of lithium metal was added in small pieces (17 mg + 34 mg + 13 mg +
4 mg = 68 mg), through the stoppered hole of the reaction flask, over a
period of 40 min. Insoluble material appeared after addition of the third
portion of Li and 6 mL of dry tetrahydrofuran was added. The solution
maintained a deep blue color for 60 min after addition of the last piece
of lithium at which point 296 mg (6 mmol) of iodomethane was added via
syringe through the stoppered hole of the reaction flask. The blue color
dissipated after addition of the first 10 μL of halide after which the
remaining halide was added in one portion. The mixture was stirred for 30
min at -65 °C and 321 mg (6 mmol) of solid ammonium chloride was added
through the stoppered hole. The cold finger condenser was quickly removed
(after removing the nitrogen line), emptied,returned to the reaction
flask (without the nitrogen line), and the cold bath was removed. The
stirred solution was allowed to warm to approximately -28 °C over a
period of 15 min. A water bath was placed under the reaction flask and
the reaction temperature increased to 15 °C over a period of 45 min. To
the residue was added 15 mL of water followed by 30 mL of benzene. The
phases were separated and the organic phase was dried (Na2SO4) and
concentrated in vacuo (25-45 torr at a temperature below 40 °C) to give
349 mg of a colorless liquid. The residue was purified by chromatography
over 18 g of flash silica gel (loaded and eluted with hexanes-ethyl
acetate = 2:1) with collection of 10 mL fractions. Fraction 11 was
concentrated to give 27 mg of a 10:1 molar ratio of 29a (R = H) and
starting ester 11. Fractions 12-18 were pooled and concentrated in vacuo
to give 216 mg of 29a (R = H). 216 mg (66%) of 29a (R = H) as a colorless
oil IR (neat) 3434, 1731 cm−1; 1H NMR (C6D6, 400 MHz) δ 1.35 (s, 3H),
2.35 (d, J = 22, 1H), 2.42 (d, J = 22 Hz, 1H), 3.30 (s, 3H), 3.73 (br s,
2H), 5.62 (dt, J = 9, 4 Hz, 1H), 5.8−5.9 (m, 2H); 13C NMR (C6D6, 100 MHz)
δ 26.5, 27.4, 44.9, 51.6, 66.3, 123.7, 124.2, 129.0, 135.8, 175.1; exact
mass calcd for C10H14O3 (M + Na)+ m/z 205.0840, found m/z 205.0835.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM incremental addition (Li in stage 2)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Birch Reductive Alkylation of Methyl m-(Hydroxymethyl)benzoate
Derivatives and the Behavior of o- and p-(Hydroxymethyl)benzoates under
Reductive Alkylation Conditions
