$DATUM Methyl 1,2-Dimethyl-2,5-cyclohexadien-1-carboxylate (28). This compound
was prepared for comparison by a variation of a known procedure.22 A
mixture of 300 mg (2 mmol) of ester 19 and 148 mg (2 mmol) of tert-butyl
alcohol was placed in a dry 100 mL 3-necked round bottom flask equipped
with a magnetic stir bar, a cold finger condenser topped with a nitrogen
line, a low temperature thermometer, and a septum. The system was placed
under a nitrogen atmosphere and dry tetrahydrofuran (6 mL) was added via
syringe. The septum was replaced with a gas inlet adapter attached to an
ammonia tank. The cold finger condenser was charged with dry ice-acetone
and the reaction flask was also cooled in a dry ice-acetone bath. Ammonia
(approximately 30 mL) was condensed in the reaction vessel over a period
of about 10 min and then the gas inlet adapter was replaced with a glass
stopper. The stirred solution was cooled to an internal temperature of
-65 °C and 42 mg (6.0 mmol) of lithium metal was added in small pieces
(28 mg + 14 mg = 42 mg), through the stoppered hole of the reaction
flask, over a period of 5 min. The solution maintained a deep blue color
after addition of the last piece of lithium for 15 min at which point 852
mg (6 mmol) of iodomethane was added via syringe through the stoppered
hole of the reaction flask. The blue color dissipated upon addition of
the iodomethane. The mixture was stirred for 60 min at -65 °C and 321 mg
(6 mmol) of solid ammonium chloride was added through the stoppered hole.
The cold finder condenser was quickly removed (after removing the
nitrogen line), emptied, and returned to the reaction flask (without the
nitrogen line), and the cold bath was removed. The stirred solution was
allowed to warm to approximately -25 °C over a period of 20 min. A water
bath was placed under the reaction flask and the reaction temperature
increased to 15 °C over a period of 35 min. To the residue was added 15
mL of water followed by 30 mL of benzene. The phases were separated and
the organic phase was dried (Na2SO4) and concentrated in vacuo (25-45
torr at a temperature below 40 °C) to give 288 mg of a colorless residue.
The residue was subjected to chromatography over 18 g of flash silica gel
(loaded and eluted with hexanes-ethyl acetate = 10:1) with collection of
10 mL fractions. Fraction 5 contained 61 mg of material that was mainly
diene 28. Fractions 6-10 were pooled and concentrated in vacuo to give
195 mg of pure 28. 195 mg (59%) of pure 28 IR (neat) 1733 cm−1; 1H NMR
(C6D6, 400 MHz) δ 1.42 (s, 3H), 1.69 (q, J = 1.5 Hz, 3H), 2.40 (d, J = 22
Hz, 1H), 2.50 (d, J = 22 Hz, 1H), 3.31 (s, 3H), 5.37 (br s, 1H), 5.6 (m,
2H); 13C NMR (C6D6, 400 MHz) δ 19.9, 24.3, 26.9, 47.7, 51.5, 121.4,
124.6, 129.9, 133.6, 174.5.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM incremental addition (Li in stage 2)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Birch Reductive Alkylation of Methyl m-(Hydroxymethyl)benzoate
Derivatives and the Behavior of o- and p-(Hydroxymethyl)benzoates under
Reductive Alkylation Conditions
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
